  "final_message": "I wrote the answer to `answer.json` in the repo root. No other files were changed.\n\n1. **Timestamp unit: milliseconds.** `records_in_period` in `billing/usage.py` converts the period bounds with `to_millis` (seconds \u00d7 1000) and compares them directly against `record[\"timestamp\"]`. The default timestamp is also `to_millis(ctx.clock.now())`, and a supplied timestamp is stored as-is.\n\n2. **Start inclusive, end exclusive.** The filter is `start <= record[\"timestamp\"] < end` (`billing/usage.py`). A record exactly at the period start counts toward that period. A record exactly at the period end does not; it falls into the next period.\n\n3. **Billing timing: in arrears.** `subscription_lines` in `billing/invoices.py` says licensed items are billed in advance and metered items in arrears. In `billing/engine.py`, `_renew` calls `_invoice_period(subscription, ended)` with the period that just closed, so metered usage appears on the invoice generated when that period ends, not the one that opens it.\n\nI did not run the test suite, since the question was answered from reading the code.",
